  "slug": "activation-sars-cov-46ec",
  "summary": "Recent preprints test whether learned protein and RNA representations recover interpretable structure from noisy, incomplete, or sparse inputs.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is whether compact learned representations recover biologically meaningful structure rather than merely fitting outputs. The frequency-space preprint encodes proteins as harmonic graphs of MD vibrational modes and predicts GO molecular function without sequence on SwissProt. The ESM-2 contact-graph study projects residue embeddings onto graphs and applies SoftBlobGIN with Gumbel-softmax substructure pooling to probe interpretability. The BINN preprint shows networks can match input-output mappings while failing to recover gene-to-pathway weights and pathway activations, a direct test of representational fidelity. A sparse autoencoder analysis of ESM-2 and ESM-3 reports 78% feature convergence across architectures and finds that structure tokens sharpen a shared feature vocabulary. What is new is explicit scrutiny of whether internal representations are faithful, not only predictive. The items differ in task and data type, so this is a dominant methodological trend rather than a shared result.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-05-15"
}
